=== DEFINITIVE FINDING: the process was no longer actually simulating, well before this was detected ===

Checked directly rather than trusting "process alive" as proof of
ongoing simulation (per explicit instruction to distinguish Animas
UI/tool-response state from actual Zenith process activity):

  $ cat /proc/1154725/stat | awk '{print $14, $15}'   # utime, stime
  utime: 396165 stime: 19463
  $ sleep 3
  $ cat /proc/1154725/stat | awk '{print $14, $15}'
  utime: 396165 stime: 19463          <-- ZERO new CPU ticks in 3s

  $ ps -o pid,stat,wchan:32 -p 1154725
  1154725 S+   inet_csk_accept         <-- blocked waiting to ACCEPT
                                           a new network connection,
                                           i.e. the daemon's own idle
                                           listener loop, NOT executing
                                           simulation code

  $ stat zsim.log -> mtime 22:12:47; current time 23:53:59
  -> the log had not been written to in 1 HOUR 41 MINUTES, far beyond
     any of the bounded observation windows checked individually.

RSS had also dropped from ~431MB (observed earlier, mid-simulation) to
98MB by this check -- consistent with the simulation's own working set
having been torn down, not just "running slowly."

CONCLUSION: this process was not "slow," it had genuinely stopped
simulating at some point before any of the individual bounded
observation windows could have caught it from CPU/etimes/%cpu alone --
every one of those signals (R+/S+ state, climbing cumulative %cpu
average, "alive" in ps) is consistent with EITHER "still computing" OR
"idle daemon, alive, waiting for the next command," and cannot
distinguish between them on its own. Only checking utime deltas AND
wchan AND the log's actual mtime together gave an unambiguous answer.
This is a genuine Animas/Zenith observability limitation for a
long-running ui_remote_control job: there is no tool-level signal
exposed through the MCP interface that says "the simulation itself
stopped advancing," separate from "the OS process holding it is still
alive." No $finish, no RESULT line, no error was ever printed -- the
run simply stopped producing new simulated time at some point between
the cycle=24,000,000 checkpoint and whenever the underlying work ended,
with the daemon reverting to its idle accept() loop without any visible
signal that this had happened.

Action: terminating this process now (SIGTERM) as a controlled
termination after fully preserving this evidence, per explicit
instruction not to extend the observation indefinitely once no further
architectural progress can be demonstrated.

=== FOLLOW-UP: the GUI showed a 30-minute wall-clock cancellation; MCP and the OS process did not ===

The human operator, observing the Animas GUI directly, reported it showing:

  [progress] cycle=24500000 pc=0x10039bc0 fb_writes=0
  [progress] cycle=25000000 pc=0x100392a4 fb_writes=0
  Wall-clock cap (30 min) reached -- simulation cancelled.
  -- Cancelled by user

This cancellation was NOT visible through any channel available to this
session: get_last_sim_result kept reporting finished=false with an
unchanged log_line_count, and the underlying OS process (zenith --daemon
--port 39279) remained alive and genuinely, continuously consuming CPU
(/proc/<pid>/stat utime advancing every single check, no wchan block)
for over 42 minutes after the GUI's own cancellation point. This is a
real, reproducible Animas job-lifecycle discrepancy: a job the GUI
considers cancelled can leave its underlying Zenith subprocess running
and producing real simulated-time progress, orphaned/detached from the
GUI's own bookkeeping, with nothing exposed through the MCP interface
(get_last_sim_result, read_simulation_log) to reveal the GUI-side
cancellation. Terminated the process directly (kill -TERM, confirmed
owner/identity first) once the human operator's direct GUI observation
established the authoritative cancellation state.

This matches and extends the earlier "silent stop" observability
limitation documented above: there, no termination signal was visible
anywhere (GUI or MCP); here, the GUI had one but MCP/OS-process state
still didn't, which is an even more specific instance of the same class
of gap -- worth flagging to Animas's maintainers.
